        <div class="head">
            <span class="eyebrow">The situation</span>
            <h2>You may not know what's exposed — or how.</h2>
            <p class="lede">You may not know whether the other side can see, track, infer, or access private parts of your digital life — or whether they already have. You may suspect something but not know where to look, or fear that one wrong change will make things worse. That uncertainty, in the middle of a separation, is its own kind of pressure.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
            <span class="eyebrow">What can become leverage</span>
            <h2>Ordinary access, quietly turned against you.</h2>
            <p class="lede">In a separation, ordinary access can quietly become leverage: shared accounts and devices, family plans, cloud folders, location sharing, recovery settings, and the online trails of an ordinary life. You may not know whether what the other side seems to know came from hacking, ordinary access, a setting, an app, or a trail — and that is exactly the point.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head">
            <span class="eyebrow">Why guessing is risky</span>
            <h2>Changing a few passwords is not cyber protection.</h2>
            <p class="lede">One visible problem rarely tells you the full picture, and a partial or mistimed fix can leave real exposure in place — or, in a contested separation, escalate things. This is interpretation under pressure, not a checklist.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

